            {
              "id": "70e512f4-3708-3911-af51-75f7b2cda3d3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc(sizeof(double)) and immediately dereferences the result (first use: deref) without checking if the allocation succeeded (hasNullGuardBeforeUse: False). This can lead to a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method, but this does not address the immediate risk of dereferencing a null pointer. No zero guard or overflow guard is present, but for this allocation size, the main risk is null dereference.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nAlways check the result of malloc before dereferencing the pointer. If malloc fails, it returns NULL, and dereferencing it will cause undefined behavior or a crash. Add a null check immediately after the allocation and handle the error appropriately, such as returning an error code or cleaning up resources.\n\n```c\nalpha = (double*)malloc(sizeof(double));\nif (alpha == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure: log, cleanup, and return error\n    return HIPSPARSE_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED;\n}\n// Safe to dereference alpha here\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a1959fab-9ccc-351c-b7cd-f002aa4dbffe",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable' because the result of a malloc call is used (dereferenced) immediately after allocation without checking if the allocation succeeded (i.e., without verifying that the returned pointer is not NULL). The absence of a null guard before the first use of the allocated pointer increases the risk of a null pointer dereference, which can lead to undefined behavior or program crashes. The allocation size is a single object (sizeof(hipDoubleComplex)), and there are no zero or overflow guards, but the primary risk here is the unchecked dereference. The pointer is eventually freed in the method, but this does not mitigate the risk of dereferencing a null pointer. The presence of a cast does not affect the vulnerability. This is a common and well-known issue in C/C++ memory management.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a possible null pointer dereference after memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nalpha = (hipDoubleComplex*)malloc(sizeof(hipDoubleComplex));\n*alpha = ...; // immediate dereference\n```\n\nYou should add a null check before dereferencing:\n\n```c\nalpha = (hipDoubleComplex*)malloc(sizeof(hipDoubleComplex));\nif (alpha == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or clean up\n    return HIPSPARSE_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED;\n}\n*alpha = ...; // safe to dereference\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the program does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would cause undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "89ae27d6-c5af-3edc-9394-67d4828667be",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as 'Likely Vulnerable: malloc result used without null check (possible null deref)', and the call verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating a high-confidence, actionable issue. The code allocates memory using malloc(sizeof(float)) and immediately dereferences the result (first use: deref) without checking if the allocation succeeded (hasNullGuardBeforeUse: False). This can lead to a null pointer dereference if malloc fails. The presence of a cast does not mitigate the risk. The pointer is freed in the method and also passed to another call, but these do not affect the core vulnerability. No zero guard or overflow guard is present, but for this allocation size, the main risk is null dereference. Therefore, this is a genuine vulnerability.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nTo prevent a possible null pointer dereference after memory allocation, always check the result of malloc before using the allocated memory. For example, if your original code is:\n\n```c\nalpha = (float*)malloc(sizeof(float));\n*alpha = 1.0f;\n```\n\nYou should add a null check before dereferencing:\n\n```c\nalpha = (float*)malloc(sizeof(float));\nif (alpha == NULL) {\n    // Handle allocation failure, e.g., return error or clean up\n    return HIPSPARSE_STATUS_ALLOC_FAILED;\n}\n*alpha = 1.0f;\n```\n\nThis ensures that if malloc fails and returns NULL, the code does not attempt to dereference a null pointer, which would cause undefined behavior or a crash.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [CWE-690: Unchecked Return Value to NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/690.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
