            {
              "id": "9c617f9a-8d6c-3592-b8ac-01799a1303d5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to a vulnerability if the string is not null-terminated. The `strlen` function counts characters until it encounters a null character. If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen` will continue reading memory beyond the intended boundary, which can lead to buffer overflows, crashes, or other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. This can be done by initializing your strings with a null character or by manually adding a null character at the end of your string. Also, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen` such as `strnlen`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read beyond this length.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of:\n\n```cpp\nlen = strlen(name);\n```\n\nYou can use:\n\n```cpp\nlen = strnlen(name, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of your string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen` function is part of the C standard library, so you need to include the `cstring` header:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n```\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-126)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS140021"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d9625be7-90b6-306e-baca-f22aa15f527b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value to seed randomness can lead to predictable random numbers, which is a security vulnerability. This is because if an attacker can predict the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated, which can lead to various types of attacks, such as brute force attacks.\n\nIn the given code example, `srand(0)` is used to seed the random number generator. This is a static value, which means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated. This makes the random numbers predictable, which is a security vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. One way to do this is to use the current time, but this is still predictable to some extent. A better way is to use a value that is more difficult for an attacker to predict, such as the process ID or a value from a hardware random number generator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix that uses the process ID to seed the random number generator:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    srand(getpid() ^ time(NULL));\n    // rest of the code\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- cstdlib\n- ctime\n- unistd.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
