            {
              "id": "7b453b95-6786-3809-9611-28b7e61f402e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the `fopen` function is used, which can lead to vulnerabilities if the file name is not properly validated or controlled. An attacker could potentially manipulate the file name to gain unauthorized access to files, or to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to the `fopen` function, such as `fopen_s` in C11, which includes additional parameters for the size of the buffer. Additionally, always validate and sanitize user input to prevent an attacker from manipulating the file name. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer alternative using `fopen_s`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <string.h>\n\nerrno_t err;\nFILE* tmpf = NULL;\nerr = fopen_s(&tmpf, r.c_str(), \"r\");\nif (err != 0) {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `<stdio.h>` for `fopen` function\n- `<string>` for `std::string` and its `c_str` method\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "c3009b10-ec97-3916-a86e-5a8900889e2e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or other types of security issues. In the provided code snippet, the `getenv` function is used, which can lead to security vulnerabilities. This function returns a pointer to the value of the environment variable, but this value can be controlled or manipulated by an attacker, leading to potential security risks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using insecure C library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. For the `getenv` function, consider using a method that allows for better control and validation of the environment variable. Always validate and sanitize inputs, even those coming from seemingly trustworthy sources like environment variables.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib>\n#include <string>\n\nconst char* matrices_dir_raw = std::getenv(\"HIPSPARSE_CLIENTS_MATRICES_DIR\");\nif (!matrices_dir_raw) {\n    // Handle the error, e.g., by throwing an exception, returning, etc.\n}\nstd::string matrices_dir = matrices_dir_raw;\n// Now you can use matrices_dir safely.\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header defines several general purpose functions, including dynamic memory management, random number generation, communication with the environment, integer arithmetics, searching, sorting and converting.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
