            {
              "id": "4a6e5568-8f80-3010-b593-1fda962a428d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Prohibited C Function Identified\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and integer overflows. In this case, the `fprintf` function is used, which can lead to format string vulnerabilities if the format string contains user-supplied data.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using C functions that do not perform bounds checking or format string checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that are provided by the language. In this case, you can use `fputs` or `fwrite` instead of `fprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* message = \"Failed to open matrix file because it does not exist. Please download the matrix file using the install script with -c flag.\";\nfputs(message, stderr);\n```\n\nIn this fixed version, `fputs` is used instead of `fprintf`. This prevents any format string vulnerabilities because `fputs` does not interpret the string as a format string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- stdio.h\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-134: Use of Externally-Controlled Format String](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/134.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
